I'd start by naming the structure out loud before touching CSS: two columns — a fixed-width sidebar and a flexible content column — and three rows in that content column — header, main content, footer — with the sidebar spanning all three rows.

```css
.dashboard {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 60px 1fr 40px;
  grid-template-areas:
    "sidebar header"
    "sidebar content"
    "sidebar footer";
  height: 100vh;
}
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
.header  { grid-area: header; }
.content { grid-area: content; overflow-y: auto; }
.footer  { grid-area: footer; }
```

The part people get wrong: the independent scroll isn't a Grid feature at all — it's `height: 100vh` on the container to give it a bounded height, plus `overflow-y: auto` on just the content area, so the sidebar and header stay put while only that region scrolls. Recognizing that the follow-up question is an overflow/height concern rather than another Grid property is exactly what's being evaluated once the base layout is up.

**Testing:** whether you can compose Grid with ordinary box-model properties instead of assuming Grid solves everything about the layout.

It happens because a nested grid is, by default, its own independent grid — the card's internal rows size themselves to that card's own content, with zero awareness of what the sibling card's rows are doing. If one card's title wraps to two lines and another's doesn't, their price rows land at different heights even though both cards sit in the same outer grid row.

The fix is `subgrid`: setting `grid-template-rows: subgrid` (and/or columns) on the nested grid tells it to inherit the parent's tracks instead of defining its own, so every card's internal rows snap to the same shared row boundaries across the whole card grid.

```css
.card {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: subgrid;
  grid-row: span 3; /* image, title, price rows from the parent */
}
```

Before subgrid landed in evergreen browsers, the workaround was uglier — measuring rendered heights in JS and forcing equal heights, or accepting the misalignment. I'd check current browser support against the project's baseline before committing to it, since it's a newer addition than the rest of Grid, but where it's available it solves a real, previously-unsolvable alignment problem cleanly.

**Testing:** whether you've hit a genuine Grid limitation in production, not just the tutorial-level feature set.
